Comment to 1712.
----------
For disk details, here's another bit out of HyPy which uses 
FilStatusDevice:...
getDev:("LOC::A:\")
...
proc getDev:(path$)
  REM Assign Dsize&, Dfree& and Dmedia& for the disk
  REM specified in path$.
  REM P_DINFO struct, 64 bytes:
  local vers%,media%,remov%,size&,free&
  local vo,lu,na,me,batt%,sp,ar

  Zts$=path$+CHR$(0)
  REM FilStatusDevice:
  if CALL($0A87,ADDR(Zts$)+1,ADDR(vers%),0,0,0)
    gIPRINT "Can't access disk"
    Dsize&=0  REM signal to calling proc.
  else
    Dsize&=size&
    Dfree&=free&
    Dmedia%=media%
  endif
endp
...
medTy%=Dmedia% AND $F
...
medTy% has values 1..6, corresponding to:

  MedTys$(1)="Floppy"
  MedTys$(2)="Hard Disk"
  MedTys$(3)="Flash"
  MedTys$(4)="Ram"
  MedTys$(5)="Rom"
  MedTys$(6)="Protected"

Note that if an SSD is "Protected", i.e. the write-protect switch is on, 
then you can't tell if it's RAM or Flash.


> 2) Is there in any benefit in purchasing the basic SDK?

For OPL programming, the main part that's useful is the "EPOC O/S System 
Services" manual from Vol.2 of the SDK.  This is roughly 150 pages (about 
a tenth of the whole SDK documentation).  To go with this, you also need 
some of the include-files (to explain structures and constants) which are 
provided on floppies with the SDK.

In both these cases Psion *could* upload everything to CIX - hint, hint, 
David - can you persuade TPTB to let you? :)

Most of the rest of the SDK is only relevant to C, but there are several 
short sections explaining file formats, programming conventions etc., 
which are generally useful.

Mike.


